# DECISIONS.log

One structured entry per phase. Consolidated into INTERVIEW.md and cleared at
each checkpoint (end of Phases 4, 9, 13, 17, 22).

Cleared at the Phase 22 checkpoint — Phases 18 through 22 now live in
INTERVIEW.md (§9 concurrency, §13 decisions, §14 questions, §15 weaknesses,
§17 claims, §19 state).

## Phase 22b — Razorpay adapter, and the field-surface audit / 2026-09-02 / (this commit)

### Built
- `src/payments/`: `razorpayProvider.ts` (authorise/capture/void/refund behind
  Phase 15c's `PaymentProvider`), `razorpayOrders.ts`, `razorpayClient.ts`
  (OK/REFUSED/UNREACHABLE, swappable transport), `razorpaySignature.ts` (webhook
  HMAC + checkout handshake), `razorpayEvents.ts`, `gatewayState.ts` (the rank
  scale), `reconciliationDecision.ts` (pure), `razorpayPayouts.ts`,
  `selectProvider.ts`.
- `src/commit/`: `razorpayWebhook.ts`, `gatewayTransition.ts`,
  `paymentReconciliation.ts`, `payoutExecution.ts`, `technicianBoard.ts`,
  `technicianEarnings.ts`.
- Routes: `POST /webhooks/razorpay`, `POST /jobs/:id/checkout`,
  `POST /jobs/:id/checkout/callback`, `GET /vehicles`,
  `GET /technician/earnings`; `/technician/work` re-modelled.
- Tables: `gateway_events` (unique on `(provider, event_id)`),
  `payments.gateway_order_ref`, `payouts.provider_ref/failure_reason/sent_at`,
  `technicians.payout_fund_account_ref`. Ledger gains `platform:bank` and
  `payoutSettlementLines`.
- UI: driver page rebuilt (stepper, vehicle picker, hero card); technician page
  rebuilt (rung track, estimate, part-sourcing/destination context, release
  notice, earnings); dispatcher action queue; `tokens.css` remapped onto
  Bootstrap 5.3's `data-bs-theme`, split into console/field/driver/technician.
- Tests: `razorpayAdapter`, `razorpayWebhook`, `razorpayPayouts`,
  `concurrency/payments`. 854 fast + 12 concurrency, all green.

### Decisions
- Decision: our own `payments.gateway_order_ref` is the authority for whether an
  order exists, passed back in as `knownOrderRef`.
  Why: Stage 2 measured `GET /v1/orders?receipt=X` returning count 0 for an
  order created seconds earlier with that receipt.
  Alternative: trust the vendor's receipt filter. Why not: it opened a second
  order on every retry — one job, two checkout screens.
  Tradeoff: a lost write between creating an order and storing its ref orphans
  an unpaid order. Costs nothing (no money is held) and is not detectable.
- Decision: `AWAITING_CUSTOMER` added to `AuthoriseResult`; `refund` added as an
  OPTIONAL interface method.
  Why: Razorpay cannot hold money synchronously, and has no void endpoint — a
  captured payment is released only by refunding.
  Alternative: reuse UNREACHABLE. Why not: it means "we never got an answer",
  which is a different operational fact.
  Tradeoff: the interface grew. No business rule moved — Phase 15c's suite
  passes unchanged, which is the check that matters.
- Decision: the reconciliation sweep, not the webhook, is the correctness
  mechanism. See `reconciliationDecision.ts`.
- Decision: technician-facing wording is a server-side whitelist
  (`technicianUpdates.ts`), mirroring `driverUpdates.ts`.
  Why: the technician page was rendering the dispatcher's raw audit panel.
  Alternative: a formatter with a `default:` branch. Why not: the failure mode
  of forgetting becomes SCREAMING_CASE on a phone.

### Concurrency
- Scenario: a driver taps Pay now while the dispatch saga is still committing.
  What races: `authoriseCallout` from `POST /jobs/:id/checkout` and from
  `authoriseCalloutQuietly`, both read-then-create on `payments`.
  Why dangerous: the loser died on `idempotency_key` and returned a 500 for a
  payment that had in fact been created correctly.
  How handled: catch the unique violation and re-read the winner's row — the
  shape `postTransaction` already used. Prisma's `upsert` was tried FIRST and
  rejected by the new test: it compiles to select-then-insert, not
  `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT`, so three concurrent callers still collided.
  Assumption relied on: the unique index on `payments.idempotency_key`.
- Scenario: Razorpay redelivers a webhook. Handled by inserting the
  `gateway_events` row in the same transaction as the transition it causes.
- Scenario: `payment.captured` arrives before `payment.authorized`. Handled by
  `movesForward` — a transition applies only if it moves the payment forward.

### Weaknesses introduced
- Payout execution is verified against a SCRIPTED rail only. RazorpayX
  credentials reach the API (`GET /v1/payouts` returns 200 with the configured
  account number) but no payout has been sent. Matters: yes, at any real scale.
- `payment_capture: false` is UNVERIFIED against a completed checkout. If the
  account auto-captures, the callout fee is taken at checkout and there is
  nothing left to capture on completion — the two-stage split becomes fiction.
  Matters: yes. It is the single largest untested assumption in the phase.
- A 401 from Razorpay still surfaces to callers as UNREACHABLE. It is now
  logged distinctly, but the retry sweep cannot tell a credential problem from
  an outage and will keep retrying. Does not matter at current scale.
- The reconciliation sweep is unbounded per pass beyond `SWEEP_LIMIT = 25`; a
  long outage clears at 25 payments per five minutes.
- The dispatcher readability pass is PARTIAL — see below.

### What broke
- The audit's own finding: the previous UI round was DRIVER-PAGE-ONLY. The
  dispatcher got three lines (`data-bs-theme`, a stylesheet link). Reported
  rather than quietly fixed.
- `GET /v1/orders?receipt=` does not filter. Found on the first live call.
- The payment race above — found by clicking Pay now on a real page, not by
  the fast suite, which calls `authoriseCallout` once.
- Bootstrap 5.3's `.btn-outline-*` emits a literal `color`, so remapping
  `--bs-btn-color` left outline buttons blue while the variable read amber.
  Only a screenshot caught it.
- `MARUTI_SUZUKI` reached both the driver's vehicle picker and the technician's
  job card. Now `src/domain/vehicleWords.ts`, shared.
- `data-panel="job-events"` on the technician page rendered `jobDetail.js` —
  the dispatcher's raw JSON audit panel — on a field phone.

### Concepts now genuinely in use
HMAC request signing over a raw body; vendor idempotency (three different
mechanisms in one integration); the dual-write problem and convergent
reconciliation as its only real answer; a monotonic state rank for out-of-order
message delivery; Bootstrap 5.3 colour modes; WCAG 2.2 target sizing.

### Interview angle
"Your payment gateway returns success and your database write is lost. What
happens?" — answerable with the reconciliation worker and, more importantly,
with why a webhook is not sufficient. And: "how did you find the concurrency bug
in your own payment path?" — by adding a second caller and clicking the button.

## Phase 22c — the CRITICAL cut, and the dispatcher readability pass / 2026-09-03 / (this commit)

### Built
- `src/domain/healthLadder.ts` — DISPATCH_CORE's `fallback` sentence rewritten.
- `public/dispatcher.html` restructured into four regions; `public/css/console.css`
  gains the region rules. `public/js/panels/queue.js` patches rows by
  `data-job-id`; new `public/js/panels/stats.js`. No route, table or engine
  change — see below.

### Decisions

**Decision** Cut the manual-assignment workflow Phase 17 specifies, and correct
the sentence that promised it, rather than build it.
**Why** `healthLadder.ts`'s DISPATCH_CORE fallback told an operator, mid-outage,
that "the queue stays visible and assignments are made by hand". The first
clause is true. The second described a screen that has never existed: the
per-row unit dropdown that would have been it was deliberately removed at
Phase 9, when the board moved from picking units one job at a time to choosing
between whole plans. Phase 22b's audit found the promise still standing.
**Alternative** Build a manual assignment path — a unit picker per queue row,
writing a hold and a commit directly, bypassing the solver.
**Why not** Three reasons, in order of weight. (1) It reintroduces exactly the
greedy per-job assignment Phase 9 removed, and it would sit in the codebase
permanently for a mode that lasts as long as a Redis outage. (2) CRITICAL
recovers on its own inside one fifteen-second probe cycle; the workflow's whole
audience is a dispatcher in a window that closes by itself. (3) It would need
its own hold/commit path around the saga — the one piece of this system where a
second write path is genuinely dangerous.
**Tradeoff** During CRITICAL nothing new is assigned at all; a dispatcher can
read the queue and talk to people, and that is the whole of it. Accepted, and
now stated on the banner instead of being contradicted by it. The build plan's
own §1546 wording is left unedited as the historical spec.

**Decision** The action queue and promotion cards live in the LEFT (monitoring)
column, not the right-hand investigation column.
**Why** Checked against the real data rather than assumed. `GET /actions`
returns every PENDING row across all jobs ordered by tier then age, and a solve
raises `APPROVE_CROSS_TENANT_CONTENTION` on whichever jobs lost a contested
unit — so in a fifty-job board the queue routinely spans jobs the dispatcher has
not selected. Promotions are the same shape: they arrive over the zone room for
any job in the dispatcher's geography. Both answer "what needs me now", which is
the left column's question.
**Alternative** The right column, filtered to the selected job.
**Why not** Filtering would hide pending approvals for every other job, which is
a functional regression dressed as a layout change; not filtering would put
cross-job rows in a column whose header says it is about one job.
**Tradeoff** The left column is now two lists deep and is the densest part of
the page. Correct for the column a dispatcher lives in.

**Decision** Competing plans stay in the CENTRE (board) region, beside Solve —
not in the right-hand column.
**Why** A plan is a statement about the whole board: `POST /plans/:id/select`
records a decision for every assignment in it. Putting it in a column that is
empty until a job is selected would mean a dispatcher could not press Solve and
take a plan without first clicking an unrelated job.
**Alternative** Follow the brief exactly and move plans right.
**Why not** It collides with the brief's own rule 1 — nothing job-scoped renders
until a job is selected — because plans are not job-scoped.
**Tradeoff** The override reason input is therefore in the centre while the
recorded override reason renders in the right column's inspector. Two places,
one subject. Acceptable: one is an input about a plan, the other is a fact about
a job.

### Concurrency
Nothing new. This phase adds no route, no write and no queue. The one behavioural
change is that `queue.js` patches rows instead of rebuilding the table, which
removes a DOM rebuild racing an operator's selection — a UI race, not a data one.

### Weaknesses introduced
- The right column's sections are stacked, not tabbed. On a 1280×800 laptop with
  a job selected and many rejected options, it scrolls. Judged better than tabs,
  which hide the rejected list behind a click when it is the second most useful
  thing on the screen — but it is a real limit at this width.
- The stat strip's three numbers are derived in the browser from state the page
  already holds, not read from an endpoint. They cannot disagree with the panels
  below them (same source), but they are the first numbers on this console not
  computed server-side. If a fourth consumer ever needs them, they should move.
- The queue's row patching keys on `data-job-id` and re-sorts by removing and
  re-appending rows in order. A queue whose ORDER changes still moves under the
  operator; only the rebuild-on-every-tick is fixed. Does not matter at current
  scale — order changes when safety or SLA changes, which is rare and is
  exactly when moving is correct.

### Concepts now genuinely in use
Keyed DOM reconciliation applied to a sorted list (as opposed to the timeline's
append-only case); region-based information architecture as a substitute for a
component library; correcting a user-facing claim as the cheaper half of a
build-or-cut decision.

### Interview angle
"Your degraded-mode banner promised a screen that did not exist. How did that
happen and what did you do?" — answerable with the Phase 9 removal, the Phase 17
spec written before it, and the choice to correct the sentence rather than
rebuild a workflow whose audience is a fifteen-second window. And: "how do you
decide what goes where on an operator console?" — monitoring versus
investigating, tested against which panels are actually job-scoped, which is how
the action queue ended up on the other side from where the brief put it.

