## Phase 22e — two scoping bugs, and the question of what commits an assignment / 2026-09-07 / (this commit)

### Built
`src/auth/permissions.ts` — `vehicleFilingFilter(driverId)`, beside
`jobReadOwnershipFilter` and shaped like it: a Prisma `where` fragment saying
which vehicles a driver may report a breakdown on. Read by `GET /vehicles`
(`src/api/vehicles.ts`) and by `POST /jobs/intake` (`src/api/intake.ts`).

`public/js/pages/driver.js` — `openJob(jobId)`, the one description of what it
means for a job to become the one this page is showing: set it active,
subscribe to its room, refetch the view, load its messages. All three entry
points — create, restore-the-only-open-job, pick-from-the-resume-list — now go
through it.

`tests/ownership.test.ts` — six tests: the picker offers a driver only their own
vehicle and not a colleague's ON THE SAME TENANT, a fleet manager and a
dispatcher are unchanged, intake still refuses what the picker no longer offers,
and `vehicleFilingFilter` fails closed.

`WALKTHROUGH.md` — running `scripts/demo-open-shifts.ts` promoted from a caveat
to Section 0, with the measured reason and a query for diagnosing a starved
board mid-run.

### Decisions

**Decision** The vehicle rule takes a DRIVER ID, not an auth context — unlike
`jobReadOwnershipFilter` beside it.
**Why** The two callers know the subject driver by different routes. The picker
knows it from the token; intake knows it from the token OR from `driverId` in
the body, because a dispatcher may file on somebody's behalf. The rule is about
the driver, so the driver is the argument.
**Alternative** Take the context and let the function work out the subject.
**Why not** It would have to know about on-behalf-of filing, which is a route
concern, and the dispatcher case would need a second parameter anyway.
**Tradeoff** Each caller still decides WHETHER there is a subject driver — one
branch in `GET /vehicles`, none in intake. That branch is about who is asking,
not about what the rule is, so it is in the right place; but it does mean the
function cannot be applied blindly.

**Decision** `openJob()` rather than adding `subscribeToJob` twice.
**Why** The bug was not a missing line, it was that three things which must
always happen together were written out three times and two copies were
incomplete. Adding the missing line to both copies leaves the same shape and the
same next bug — the message thread was already missing from both, for exactly
this reason.
**Alternative** Two lines, as first scoped.
**Why not** It fixes this instance and not the class.
**Tradeoff** The create path now calls a function that also loads chat and
subscribes, which it was already doing inline; no behaviour changed there, but
its ordering is now decided somewhere else.

### Concurrency
Nothing. Both changes narrow a read; neither adds a write, a route or a queue.

### Weaknesses introduced
- `openJob()` is not covered by a test. The browser modules this repo does test
  (`offlineQueue`, `toast`, `consolePanels`, `fleetDashboard`) are panels with
  pure inputs; `driver.js` is page wiring over sockets, the API and the DOM, and
  a test for it would be mostly mock. Verified in a real browser with CDP frame
  logging instead, which is evidence and not a regression guard.
- `GET /vehicles` still has no way to ask "which vehicles may driver X file
  against" on somebody's behalf, so a dispatcher taking a call cannot be shown
  the right list. Nothing consumes that today — the picker is the endpoint's
  only caller — but the on-behalf-of intake path exists and has no matching
  read.

### What broke
**A driver who resumed a job received no live updates, and no messages.**
`setActiveJob` was called in three places; only the create path followed it with
`subscribeToJob` and `loadChat`. So a driver who signed in again, or reloaded,
held a socket that connected, reported "Connected", and joined no room — the
panel was correct at fetch time and then never changed again. Measured with CDP
frame logging: 0 `job:event` frames across a run after resuming, 15 across the
same run when the job was created in that window; after the fix, 15 and updates
landing in 19–63ms. The failure mode is the interesting part: nothing was
wrong on screen, it was only old, and a page that looks live and is not is worse
than one that admits it is offline.

**The vehicle picker offered cars intake would refuse.** `GET /vehicles` was
scoped by tenant alone while intake matched on `driverId`. The route carried a
comment claiming the scoped client narrowed a driver to their own rows; it does
not — `jobOwnershipExtension` covers the Job model only. A comment asserting an
invariant that nothing enforces is the same hazard as the duplicated rule it was
describing.

### Concepts now genuinely in use
Nothing new. Both fixes are the same old one: a rule stated twice drifts, and
the fix is one function with two callers.

### Interview angle
"What commits an assignment in your system?" — and the honest answer is that
there is exactly one door and it is not the one you would guess. `POST /solve`
and the queued zone re-plan both stop at an audit record (`persistSolverRun`);
`POST /plans/:id/select` writes `decision_records` and a `PLAN_SELECTED` event
and stops. The only production caller of `startSaga` is `executeReassignment`,
reached only from `POST /actions/:id/execute` for two `REASSIGN_*` action kinds,
and the only place a job is ever assigned is `commitSaga`. That is a wiring gap
rather than a design: `src/api/solve.ts` and `src/commit/persistSolverRun.ts`
both still carry comments saying holds and the saga are "Phase 11 and Phase 12
… until they exist", both of which shipped; `BUILD_PHASES.md`'s Phase 9 says "no
reservations yet"; and the job statuses `PLANNED` and `OFFERED` are dead —
`PLANNED` is written only by compensation and by release-on-replan, and nothing
anywhere writes `OFFERED`. Two dead states are what an unfinished happy path
looks like from the schema.

