## Phase 22g — the job says what is happening to it, and the tow fails closed / 2026-09-07 / (this commit)

### Built
`src/commit/saga.ts` — `setJobStatus()`, a conditional writer, and two calls to
it: `OFFERED` when leg 2 goes out, `AWAITING_DESTINATION` when leg 3 does.
`compensateSaga`'s guard list gains `AWAITING_DESTINATION`.

`src/commit/redispatch.ts` — `raiseNoFeasiblePlan()` extracted from
`escalateNoFeasiblePlan` so both callers share one description of "make this
dead end visible", and a guard in `executeReassignment` that refuses to start a
saga whose plan names a workshop. New outcome `DESTINATION_UNCONFIRMABLE`.

`tests/saga.test.ts` — four tests on the job status through the reservation.
`tests/redispatch.test.ts` — two on the guard, plus `withoutDestination()` and
the discovery that made it necessary.

`INTERVIEW.md` §15.41 rewritten to size the three gaps, new §15.44, §16 and the
§17 resume claim qualified. `WALKTHROUGH.md` prep block and defect list.

### Decisions

**Decision** The status writes are conditional `updateMany`s naming the statuses
they may move FROM, not `update` by id.
**Why** A slow write from an abandoned saga must never pull a committed job back
to `OFFERED`. Zero rows affected means somebody else got there first, which is
information rather than an error — the same optimistic pattern the unit hold one
leg earlier already uses.
**Alternative** A version column on Job, or a transaction around the whole leg.
**Why not** The job already has a `version` used elsewhere, and taking it here
would mean threading an expected version through `startSaga`'s signature for a
write whose only failure mode is "already further along".
**Tradeoff** A silent no-op. If the guard list is ever wrong the status simply
does not move and nothing complains — which is exactly what
`compensateSaga`'s list would have done for `AWAITING_DESTINATION` had the test
not caught it.

**Decision** `AWAITING_DESTINATION` is written even though nothing can reach it.
**Why** Asked for, and correct: the line that sets it belongs next to the line
that creates the offer, not in a future commit that has to remember. It cost one
call and it made `compensateSaga`'s missing guard entry visible immediately.
**Alternative** Leave it until a workshop counterparty exists.
**Why not** Then two things have to be remembered instead of one, and the second
is the one nobody writes down.
**Tradeoff** A line of production code with no production path, which is the
thing this project otherwise treats as a defect. Justified here only because the
guard that makes it unreachable is in the same change and says so.

**Decision** The tow guard lives in `executeReassignment`, before
`releaseCurrentCrew`, not inside `startSaga`.
**Why** Position is the whole point: a job we cannot dispatch must not also lose
the crew it already had. Refusing inside `startSaga` would happen after the
release had already run.
**Alternative** Refuse in `startSaga`, where the leg is actually created.
**Why not** `startSaga` has no way to raise an escalation without importing the
action layer, and it is called by tests that legitimately exercise the
three-leg machine.
**Tradeoff** `startSaga` still accepts a `workshopId` nothing in production
passes. Deliberate — the state machine and its tests are correct and should not
be dismantled because the actor is missing.

**Decision** `DESTINATION_UNCONFIRMABLE` is its own outcome, not a
`NOT_EXECUTABLE`.
**Why** They mean opposite things to a reader: `NOT_EXECUTABLE` is "this action
is stale or malformed", and this is "the action is perfectly well formed and the
product cannot honour it". The file already split `SAGA_LOST_RACE` out for the
same reason.
**Tradeoff** A fourth outcome on a type the API route flattens to
`reserved: false` anyway.

### Concurrency
**Scenario** Two dispatchers execute the same reassignment, or a stale action is
executed after a later saga already committed the job.
**What races** `setJobStatus` against `commitSaga`'s own status write.
**Why dangerous** An unconditional write would move a COMMITTED job back to
OFFERED, and the technician board (`OPEN_STATUSES`) would drop a job somebody is
driving to.
**How handled** The write names the statuses it may move from; the loser affects
zero rows. Asserted directly — "does not pull a committed job back to OFFERED"
starts a second saga on an already-committed job and checks the status holds.
**Assumption relied on** That `COMMITTED` and everything above it are never in
the `from` list. Nothing enforces that beyond reading it.

A second, unchanged: `raiseNoFeasiblePlan`'s `hasOpenAction` check is still a
read-then-write with a window, so two triggers in the same instant can both
raise a card. Same shape as the thrash escalation, same judgement — a duplicate
row in a worklist, not a duplicate dispatch.

### Weaknesses introduced
- `OFFERED` removes a job from `PENDING_STATUSES`, so a concurrent whole-board
  solve no longer sees it. That is a fix — a job mid-reservation should not be
  planned into a second van — but it was not the intent of the change and
  nothing tests it.
- An `OFFERED` job is also skipped by the SLA escalation sweep, which scans
  `['INTAKE','PLANNED']`. Forty-five seconds of invisibility on a job that is
  already late. Does not matter at current scale; would need naming if the
  offer TTL ever grew.
- The tow guard fires on `impact.destinationWorkshopId`, which is written when
  the RECOMMEND is raised. A plan that needed no workshop when proposed and
  would need one now is not caught — the guard reads the stored decision, not a
  fresh one. Correct for auditability and wrong if the two ever disagree.
- `startSaga`'s `workshopId` parameter now has no production caller. Dead
  surface kept alive by tests, which is the shape this project usually calls
  out.

### What broke
**Two existing tests failed, and the reason was the finding.**
`tests/redispatch.test.ts`'s fixture job requires `FLATBED_LOADING`, so
`faultsNeedTow` is true for every job in that file and candidate generation
resolves a destination for every option — which is why the fixture goes to the
trouble of creating an authorised workshop. The two tests that execute a
reassignment were therefore exercising a reservation **that could never have
committed in production**: leg 3 would have sat at PROPOSED and timed out. They
passed for four phases because they compensate by hand rather than waiting
forty-five seconds for the timeout to do it for them. Both now clear the
workshop explicitly through `withoutDestination()`, which exists mostly to write
that paragraph down where somebody will read it.

**A test caught the compensation guard I had not thought about.** Writing
`AWAITING_DESTINATION` without adding it to `compensateSaga`'s `where` list
would have left a compensated tow stranded in a status nothing else moves out
of. The assertion that a declined destination returns the job to `PLANNED` is
what surfaced it, and it is the reason that assertion is in the same test as the
status write rather than in one of its own.

### Concepts now genuinely in use
Nothing new. Conditional state transition without a version column, applied to a
second table; and failing closed as a deliberate alternative to a feature —
refusing an operation the product cannot honour, and saying why, rather than
starting it and unwinding.

### Interview angle
"You found a leg of your saga that nothing could answer. Why did you not just
auto-accept it?" — because a workshop that has not said yes has not said yes,
and the honest states are "we cannot dispatch this" and "somebody must arrange
it by phone", not a fabricated acceptance that would also poison the learned
P(accept) it feeds. And: "what is the difference between the three gaps in your
console?" — one line, one call, and one counterparty that was never specified;
being able to size them separately is most of what §15 is for.

## Phase 22h — the engine narrates itself / 2026-09-10 / (this commit)

### Built
`src/engine/trace.ts` — new. `SolveStage` (a discriminated union of seven
stages: PENDING, CANDIDATES, FEASIBILITY, SAFETY_BANDS, COST, OUTCOME, DONE),
`StageReporter`, `SafetyBandTrace`, `countRejectionsByCode()`,
`countUnassignedByReason()`, `addBand()`, `CONTENTION_REASON`. Facts only — the
file writes no prose.

`src/engine/solve.ts` — `SolveInput.onStage?`, optional and read-only.
`buildPlan()` gains an `onBand` callback and reports `unitsOffered` /
`unitsLeftBelow` as each safety band hands units down.

`src/api/solve.ts` — `POST /solve` now answers NDJSON instead of one JSON body.
`StreamMessage` (`stage` | `result` | `error`), and `planAndRecord()` split out
so the route is only the streaming contract.

`public/js/api.js` — `solveJobs(onStage)` and `eachJsonLine()`, which buffers
partial lines and flushes the TextDecoder's straddling multi-byte tail.
`authHeaders()` and `failure()` extracted from `request()`.

`public/js/state.js` — `solveTrace: { stages, running }`, `startSolveTrace()`,
`pushSolveStage()`, `finishSolveTrace()`.

`public/js/panels/solveTrace.js` — new, appends-only.
`public/js/panels/solveNarration.js` — new, `stageSentence()`.
`public/js/panels/decisionNarration.js` — new, `narrateDecision()`.
`public/js/panels/rejectionWords.js` — new, `REJECTION_PHRASES` and
`CREW_REJECTION_PHRASES` in two voices, `describeJobRejections()`,
`describeCrewRejections()`.
`public/js/panels/planWords.js` — new, `PLAN_WORDS`, `planWord()`,
`describePlans()`.

`public/js/panels/inspector.js` — `narrationBlock()` replaces `overrideBlock()`,
built with `textContent` throughout. `public/js/panels/plans.js` — the summary
strip and its private `REJECTION_PHRASES` deleted; `describeUnassigned()` now
delegates its wording.

`src/api/console.ts` — `namesForTechnicians()`, `withName()`, and
`selectedOptionId` on the decision payload. `src/commit/decisionRecords.ts` —
`latestDecisionFor()` returns `selectedOptionId`.

`public/dispatcher.html`, `public/css/console.css`,
`public/css/console-regions.css` — the trace panel and `.decision-story`, with a
`prefers-reduced-motion` branch on the line animation.

`tests/narration.test.ts` — new, 18 tests on the actual wording.
`tests/unassignedReasons.test.ts` — the two phrase maps and `PLAN_WORDS`
asserted complete against the engine. No schema change, no migration.

### Decisions

**Decision** The engine reports facts; the console owns every sentence.
**Why** One vocabulary for the decision, in the place a person reads it. The
trace and the inspector have to agree with the plan card about what
`CAPABILITY_MISSING` means, and they can only do that if the phrase exists once.
**Alternative** Have `solve()` emit finished strings.
**Why not** The engine would grow a second vocabulary that drifts from the
console's within a phase, and `src/engine` would acquire a presentation concern
it has never had.
**Tradeoff** The console has no build step, so the phrase maps cannot import the
engine's `RejectionCode` union or `WEIGHT_VECTORS`. Two tests parse the
TypeScript source and do what a compiler would.

**Decision** NDJSON over the existing POST, not a socket.
**Why** The stages belong to one request and die with it. Socket.IO is already
connected and carries board-wide events; putting a per-request transcript on it
would mean every dispatcher receives every other dispatcher's solve narration.
**Alternative** Socket.IO, or poll a run-status endpoint.
**Why not** A socket needs a correlation id and a subscription teardown to say
"this run, this operator", which is machinery for something the response body
already scopes correctly.
**Tradeoff** Headers go out with the first line, so the status code is committed
before the work starts. A failure after that point is a 200 carrying an `error`
line — see Weaknesses.

**Decision** The safety handover is reported for the recommended plan only, and
summed across zone partitions.
**Why** All four vectors solve the same bands in the same order over the same
jobs; reporting each would put one argument on screen four times about plans
nobody took. A partitioned solve runs the bands once per zone, and showing the
same band three times with numbers a reader cannot add up is worse than summing.
**Alternative** Report every vector, or report each partition separately.
**Why not** Both produce a transcript that is longer and says less.
**Tradeoff** The summed line hides that a partitioned board was several
independent matrices. The COST stage says so on its own line instead.

**Decision** A 110ms minimum beat between showing lines (`TRACE_LINE_MS` in
`public/js/pages/dispatcher.js`).
**Why** The first stages land while routing is still going and genuinely arrive
seconds apart; the last four are arithmetic over data already in memory and land
within a millisecond, which drops four sentences at once and reads as a result.
**Alternative** No delay, or an artificial delay on the server.
**Why not** Delaying on the server would make `wallClockMs` — a number the DONE
line prints — describe the pause rather than the solve.
**Tradeoff** The screen takes longer than the run. The stated runtime is the
server's real measurement, so on a fast board the last line can claim fewer
milliseconds than the transcript visibly took to appear.

**Decision** The per-job narration never says how many crews were CONSIDERED.
**Why** `persistSolverRun.ts` writes every rejected option but only the feasible
options a plan actually picked, so the feasible-but-unchosen ones were never
recorded. "Of the six who could, this one was cheapest" is a count nobody can
check.
**Alternative** Persist a per-job tally, or derive it from the stored rows.
**Why not** Deriving it undercounts — four vectors choosing one van is four rows
and one unit. Persisting it is a migration, and the number is a nicety next to
the reasons, which are all recorded exactly.
**Tradeoff** The panel answers "how many were ruled out and why" and declines
"out of how many". `tests/narration.test.ts` pins the refusal so a later edit
cannot quietly invent it.

**Decision** Weight-vector names get English words too (`planWords.js`).
**Why** `PROTECT_CONTRACTS` is the right thing to print on a plan card, where it
is a label. Inside a sentence it is jargon, and both new surfaces write prose.
**Alternative** Leave the labels raw in prose.
**Why not** The rule that turns reason codes into phrases is about sentences,
not about which enum it is.
**Tradeoff** A fourth vocabulary map to keep complete. `planWord()` falls back to
the label, so an unworded vector reads as the code — ugly and true, which is the
right way round.

**Decision** The plan card's summary strip is deleted, not kept alongside.
**Why** `25 jobs · 600 options · 318 feasible · 1400ms` is the same four figures
the trace now states with what they count. Two versions of one fact, one of which
a person must be told how to read, is one too many.
**Alternative** Keep it as a compact summary for someone who prefers density.
**Why not** It is the exact reading the brief calls bare numbers.
**Tradeoff** A returning operator who had learned the strip has to read sentences.

### Concurrency
**Scenario** A dispatcher presses Solve twice before the first run finishes.
**What races** Two NDJSON streams pushing stages into one `solveTrace.stages`
array.
**Why dangerous** Not a data race — the console is single-threaded — but the
transcript would interleave two runs into one that describes neither, and the
final `setSolve` would pair the loser's plans with the winner's transcript.
**How handled** A `solving` flag in `dispatcher.js` ignores the second press.
**Assumption relied on** One console tab is one operator. Two tabs still produce
two independent runs; that was already true and the server serialises nothing.

**Scenario** The solve throws after the first stage line is written.
**What races** Nothing, but the status code is already committed.
**Why dangerous** Express's error handler would try to set 500 on a response
whose headers have gone, and the browser would get a truncated stream with no
signal that it was truncated.
**How handled** The snapshot read stays ABOVE the first `res.write`, so
everything that can fail early still fails as ordinary JSON with a status. After
that, failures are an `error` line and `api.js` throws on it; a stream that ends
with no `result` line throws too.
**Assumption relied on** `buildDispatchSnapshot` is the only pre-solve step that
can fail. If a future edit puts work between the header write and the solve, that
work loses its status code silently.

### Weaknesses introduced
- **A failed solve answers 200.** Once the stream has started the status cannot
  change, so a mid-run failure is a 200 whose body carries an `error` line. The
  browser handles it; anything watching HTTP status — a proxy, an uptime check —
  sees success. Does not matter at current scale because nothing monitors this
  route, and it would matter the moment something did.
- **The transcript is not persisted.** It lives in browser state and a reload
  loses it. The `SolverRun` row still holds the same figures, so nothing
  auditable is lost — but the sentences are not reconstructable after the fact.
- **The stated runtime and the perceived runtime differ**, because of the beat.
  The number is honest; the experience is slower than the number.
- **One extra query per decision panel open** (`namesForTechnicians`), over the
  handful of technicians a job's options name. Chosen over a join on every option
  row for a field only this panel wants.
- **Four phrase maps to keep complete.** Three are asserted against the engine by
  tests; that is the only thing making the duplication safe.
- **`describeCrewRejections` counts OPTIONS, not crews**, and the sentence says
  "crews". For one job the two coincide, which is where it is used — but the name
  and the noun would both be wrong if it were ever pointed at a whole board.

### Concepts now genuinely in use
NDJSON and chunked transfer — one flushed JSON object per line, read
incrementally with a `ReadableStream` reader, a line buffer, and a streaming
`TextDecoder`. First place in the project where a response is consumed before it
is complete. Backpressure is NOT handled: `res.write` returns a boolean nothing
reads, which is safe only because a transcript is a handful of small lines.

### What broke
Nothing broke in the wiring — the stream, the stages and both panels worked as
built. Two things were wrong on review.

The plan labels were printed as raw enums inside prose: `The FASTEST_RESOLUTION
plan would have sent…`, and a comma-joined list of all four in the COST line. The
rule about reason codes had been followed exactly and read as being only about
reason codes. The fix is `planWords.js` plus a completeness test that parses
`WEIGHT_VECTORS` out of `src/engine/cost.ts` — there was a test asserting every
`RejectionCode` has words and no equivalent for the vectors, which is why nothing
caught it. That test also asserts a word is not merely the label echoed back,
because `planWord()`'s fallback returns the label and a map entry that copied the
code would otherwise pass.

Verification was blocked for most of the session: Docker was not running, so the
DB-backed suites could not run and `tests/solve.test.ts`'s invariant-2 suite
failed on connection rather than on anything real. Worth stating plainly because
the pure narration tests pass without a database and it would have been easy to
call the work verified on their evidence alone. With Postgres, Redis and OSRM up:
996 fast tests and 12 concurrency tests pass, typecheck and lint clean.

### Interview angle
"Your solver is a pure function over a snapshot — how did you make it narrate
itself without breaking that?" A `StageReporter` callback the engine calls and
never reads, so a run with a reporter returns exactly the plans a run without one
returns and the evaluation harness, which passes none, is unaffected.

"You say safety is lexicographic rather than a weight. Prove it." The
SAFETY_BANDS line prints how many units each band was offered and how many it
left below: no amount of cost can move a unit upward across those two numbers,
because by the time the lower band is solved the unit is already gone.

