## Phase 22i — cutting a surface, and a requeue that told the driver the opposite of the truth / 2026-10-14 / (this commit)

### Built
Nothing new was built. One surface was removed and one bug was fixed.

REMOVED — the fleet manager's dashboard, all of it reachable only from
`GET /fleet/dashboard`: `public/fleet.html`, `public/js/pages/fleet.js`,
`public/js/panels/fleetDashboard.js`, `src/api/fleet.ts` (`fleetRouter`),
`src/commit/fleetDashboard.ts` (`buildFleetDashboard`, `toActiveIncidentView`,
`groupByVehicle`), `src/commit/fleetIncidents.ts` (`fleetIncidentsFor`,
`isActiveIncident`, `FleetIncident`), `src/domain/fleetMetrics.ts`
(`slaMetSummary`, `currentExposurePaise`, `meanResolutionSummary`,
`spendPaiseThisMonth`, `isActive`, `startOfMonthUtc`),
`src/domain/fleetSlaOutcome.ts` (`slaOutcomeFor`), `tests/fleetDashboard.test.ts`.
Plus the `/fleet` nav card, the `/fleet` static page route, the `fleetDashboard`
permission resource, and the two orphaned frontend helpers
(`getFleetDashboard`, `setFleetDashboard`). `tests/fleetDomain.test.ts` trimmed
315 to 94 lines.

FIXED — `src/domain/driverNarrative.ts` (`headlineFor`) now branches the PLANNED
sentence on `helperName`; `src/domain/actionTiers.ts` gains
`ESCALATE_DISPATCH_REQUEUED` (RECOMMEND, in `CHANGES_WHO_IS_GOING`);
`src/commit/saga.ts` (`finishAsRequeued`) raises that card;
`public/js/panels/actions.js` gains its label. New:
`tests/requeueNarration.test.ts` (7 tests).

### Decisions

Decision: cut the fleet dashboard UI and API, keep the tenancy in the engine.
Why: BUILD_PHASES.md's own "IF YOU FALL BEHIND" list names it as cut #2 in
exactly those words, and the cluster turned out to be strictly self-contained —
`buildFleetDashboard` had one importer, `fleetIncidentsFor` one, `slaOutcomeFor`
one, and nothing in `evaluation/`, `scripts/` or `prisma/` referenced any of it.
Alternative: remove the whole FLEET_MANAGER concept. Why not: the role is woven
through auth, permissions, job ownership, realtime rooms and the notification
worker, and removing it would take cross-tenant contention and per-tenant row
scoping with it — the strongest tenancy material in the repo, and all of it
engine-side rather than dashboard-side. Tradeoff: the "fourth projection of the
same event stream" claim is gone, and `src/domain/repeatPattern.ts` is now a
tested rule with no caller.

Decision: three files beyond the literal brief went too — `fleetIncidents.ts`,
`fleetMetrics.ts`, `fleetSlaOutcome.ts`. Why: they are the handler's exclusive
data and calculation layer with zero other consumers, so keeping them would
leave dead code that the docs would then have to either explain or lie about.
Alternative: keep them against a future surface. Why not: nothing is scheduled,
and git is the place unused code waits.

Decision: fix the requeue bug in the NARRATION, not in the status. Why:
`helperName` was already in `NarrativeContext` and is the durable fact, so the
honest branch costs no new backend and no migration. Alternative: write a
different JobStatus from `finishAsRequeued`. Why not: `PLANNED` is read as
pending by `dispatchSnapshot.ts`'s `PENDING_STATUSES` and by the console queue,
so changing it would move the job out of the solver's own candidate set in order
to fix a sentence.

Decision: `ESCALATE_DISPATCH_REQUEUED` does not restart a saga. Why: choosing a
new crew is a re-solve, which is `handleTrigger`'s job and needs a plan this card
does not carry; Execute means "I have seen it", exactly as it does on the two
escalation cards beside it. Alternative: put the kind in `REASSIGNMENT_KINDS` so
executing it starts a reservation. Why not: there is no chosen unit on the card
to reserve. Tradeoff: a dispatcher still has to cause a re-solve by hand.

### Concurrency
Scenario: a saga's leg 1 loses its race, twice, for the same job.
What races: two dispatchers proposing the same unit, or one proposing against a
version another already moved. `createHold`'s conditional UPDATE affects zero
rows for the loser.
Why dangerous: the card is raised outside the transaction that writes state
REQUEUED, so two losers could each find no open card and each raise one.
How handled: `hasOpenAction` guards on `(jobId, kind, status PENDING)`, the same
guard the thrash and no-plan branches use.
Assumption relied on: that guard is read-then-write and therefore NOT atomic —
two simultaneous requeues of one job can still produce two cards. Identical in
shape to section 15.33's cross-tenant contention weakness, with the same honest
fix unbuilt (a partial unique index Prisma cannot model). Cosmetic: both cards
point at the same job and resolving either leaves the other resolvable.

### Weaknesses introduced
- `src/domain/repeatPattern.ts` has no production caller. Tested, correct, dead.
  Matters only as tidiness at this scale; recorded as INTERVIEW.md section
  15.35 so it is not discovered as a surprise.
- `ESCALATE_DISPATCH_REQUEUED` can double up under a simultaneous double
  requeue, above.
- The Phase 15c coverage-cap booking gap is now UNMASKED rather than fixed.
  `fleetSpend` used to read the approved estimate total and so hid it; that
  reader is gone, and the bug it was sidestepping is still there. Section 15.34
  rewritten to say so.
- `tests/requeueNarration.test.ts` leaves one CANCELLED job per run behind in
  `zippy_test` forever, because `job_events` is append-only by trigger and the
  rows cannot be deleted. It ages the previous run's job rather than growing a
  pending backlog, which is the best available shape, not a good one.

### Concepts now genuinely in use
Nothing new. The requeue card reuses the existing action-tier table and the
`hasOpenAction` dedupe; the narration fix is a branch on data already in hand.
Worth naming instead: scoping an assertion to rows the test itself created,
which is the only thing that made the invariant-8 test provable at all.

### What broke
The invariant-8 test was wrong twice before it was right, and both failures were
more interesting than the fix.

First it looked for `job.jobId` in `GET /queue`'s response, which returns `id`.
That was my error, not the product's.

Then it still failed, and the reason was the real finding. `/queue` does select
crewless PLANNED jobs — `status in (INTAKE, PLANNED)` and `assignedUnitId: null`
— but it returns `orderQueue(...).slice(0, 50)`, and `zippy_test` holds 9,274
pending unassigned jobs accumulated from every previous run. A NORMAL job two
minutes old sorts below every HIGH_RISK one and is nowhere near the top fifty.
So the honest statement of the bug is sharper than the one I started with: a
requeued job was not merely narrated wrongly, it reached an operator only once
`ESCALATE_SLA_WINDOW_CLOSED` fired — after the promise was already broken. That
is what made the card the fix rather than a nicety, and it is CLAUDE.md's
shared-database gotcha arriving as a product finding rather than as flakiness.

The cleanup then hit the append-only trigger: `jobs.deleteMany` is refused with
`P0001: append-only table job_events: DELETE is not permitted`, because the saga
writes two events. Hence per-run ids and ageing instead.

Separately, `src/engine/solve.ts` was found to contain three raw NUL bytes used
as a composite-map-key separator, written as literal control characters rather
than the six-character escape. `file` reports the source as `data` and ripgrep
refuses to show matching lines in it, so searching that file silently returns
"Binary file matches". Pre-existing at HEAD, not touched: out of scope, and the
rules for this work barred `src/engine`.

### Interview angle
"You removed a feature. How did you know it was safe?" By reading imports rather
than grepping names: the intake prior's two modules import only `domain/faults`
and `domain/fleetPrior`, so the thing Phase 21 is actually checked on — a fleet
vehicle asking fewer intake questions — never touched the dashboard at all.

"Invariant 8 says every degraded state is visible to an operator. Was it?" No —
and the test is what proved it. A requeued job sat in a 50-row slice of 9,274
pending rows with nothing marking it, so the first thing that spoke was the SLA
breach card. Visible only after it becomes a breach is not visible.

## Phase 26 — a self-maintaining demo board on Railway / 2026-10-17 / (this commit)

### Built
- `src/demo/demoCrews.ts` — the demo cast as plain data: zone, dispatcher,
  driver, four crews (A Janpath JUMP_START only; B Gole Market, both battery
  skills + BAT-45AH; C Barakhamba flatbed, both skills, no stock; D Hauz Khas,
  four skills, fully stocked). `DEMO_DRIVER_LOCATION` = the driver form's
  default coordinates.
- `src/demo/busyDemoUnits.ts` `findBusyUnitIds`, `src/demo/idleDemoCrews.ts`
  `findIdleDemoCrews`, `src/demo/refreshDemoShifts.ts`,
  `src/demo/refreshDemoStock.ts`.
- `prisma/seed/demoBoard.ts` `ensureDemoBoard`, `prisma/seed/demoCrewRows.ts`
  `seedDemoCrew`; `prisma/seed/demoMinimal.ts` reduced to the CLI entry.
- `src/workers/demoRefresh.ts` — 15-minute job scheduler on the cleanup queue,
  removed when DEMO_MODE is off; `src/worker.ts` runs it once on boot.
- `scripts/start-process.sh` — web runs ensureDemoBoard after migrations when
  `DEMO_MODE=true`.
- config: `DEMO_MODE`, `ROUTING_MODE`, `chooseRoutingMode`; `/health` gains
  `routing: { mode, detail }`; `src/infra/routing.ts` short-circuits to
  haversine in HAVERSINE mode.
- `src/infra/waitForMigrations.ts` — `waitUntilApplied`, `missingMigrations`.
- `mountChaosRoutes(app, isProduction)`; `seedPassword(env)`.
- `scripts/demo-dry-check.ts` rewritten around `solve()`, exits 1 unless B;
  now shipped in the image.
- `prisma/cutover/wipe-all-data.sql`, `docs/LIVE-CUTOVER.md`,
  `docs/DEPLOYMENT.md` rewritten, DEMO.md rewritten for four crews.
- Tests: demoBoard, seedPassword, routingMode, productionRoutes,
  waitForMigrations; health gains a routing assertion.
- Live: Railway `osrm` service deleted, `OSRM_URL` removed from web and worker.

### Decisions
Decision: four crews, each failing a different filter, instead of one van.
Why: with one van the solver has no choice to show; A, C and D make a hard
filter, a parts filter and a real cost trade-off each visible.
Alternative: one capable van. Why not: nothing to explain on the inspector.
Tradeoff: the outcome now depends on cost weights, so it needs a check.

Decision: when the dry check chose D, move D (the seed), not the cost function.
Why: the engine is what the evaluation harness measures; bending it for a demo
makes the demo a lie. Alternative: strip D's extra skills. Why not: D's wider
coverage is the product's thesis and worth showing losing to distance honestly.
Tradeoff: D's position encodes a margin (B wins by ~14 cost points on
haversine, ~4.7 on OSRM roads) that a cost-weight change could erase — the dry
check is what notices.

Decision: busy vans are skipped, not refused, by the seed.
Why: it now runs on every web boot; a refusal would stop the site starting.
Alternative: keep refusing. Why not: one stuck job takes the site down.

Decision: refresh STOCK as well as shifts every 15 minutes.
Why: counts older than 8h read UNVERIFIED, which the parts layer treats as
absent — B's battery would vanish overnight and D would win.
Alternative: only shifts, as asked. Why not: the board breaks within a day.
Tradeoff: a fixture pretending a recount happened, gated by DEMO_MODE.

Decision: periodic refresh as a BullMQ job scheduler, not a timer.
Why: the health loop documents itself as the one deliberate bare timer, and the
cleanup queue is the periodic-work queue. Schedulers persist in Redis, so
DEMO_MODE=false removes it explicitly.

Decision: haversine as the explicit production default (ROUTING_MODE).
Why: OSRM was the biggest always-on cost; an explicit mode avoids paying
OSRM_TIMEOUT_MS per call to discover it is gone. Alternative: keep OSRM.
Why not: trial credit. Tradeoff: no one-way or divider awareness in
production; `/health` states it; development and tests keep OSRM. Deviates
from the stack line "self-hosted OSRM from Phase 6"; invariant 10 still holds.

Decision: wipe via one TRUNCATE in a reviewed SQL file a person runs.
Why: atomic, keeps `_prisma_migrations`, and gets past the append-only row
triggers precisely because it is not a row operation — which is why the app
never calls it.

Decision: drop railway.json the same day it was added. Why: Railway stops
reading config-as-code on 2026-12-01, and it only restated the live default.

### Concurrency
Scenario: the 15-minute refresh (or a web boot) runs while a dispatcher holds a
demo van for an offer. What races: the refresh resetting the van to AVAILABLE
against the saga consuming the hold. Why dangerous: the van reads AVAILABLE
with a job on it and a second job lands on it — invariant 7. How handled:
`findBusyUnitIds` treats an unfinished assigned job or an unexpired ACTIVE hold
as busy, and every demo write skips busy vans. Assumption relied on: the
check-then-write window is not atomic — a hold taken between the read and the
write can still be overwritten. Accepted for a demo board; the real dispatch
path's holds are conditional updates and are unaffected.

Scenario: worker and web boot together on a deploy with new migrations.
What races: the worker's first queries against web's `migrate deploy`. How
handled: the worker waits for every shipped migration to appear finished in
`_prisma_migrations`, backoff to 15s, 5 min cap, then exits non-zero and the
platform restarts it.

### Weaknesses introduced
- The busy check is read-then-write, not a conditional update (above). Matters
  only if a reservation lands inside a refresh's few milliseconds.
- Haversine in production: ETAs ignore road shape; flagged degraded everywhere.
- The demo outcome depends on cost weights and is guarded only by the dry
  check, which is not in the test suite (it needs a demo-only database).
- The four-crew browser flow has not been walked end to end; DEMO.md marks
  which screens are engine-measured only.
- Live still runs Postgres 18 / Redis 8.2 against 16 / 7 tested; the swap is
  an untested appendix in LIVE-CUTOVER.md.
- No backups on the Railway Postgres.
- The demo crews compete with other suites' fixtures in zippy_test while
  tests/demoBoard.test.ts runs; they did win demoFlow's job once.

### Concepts now genuinely in use
Idempotent boot-time seeding on natural keys; job schedulers persisted in
Redis, and why removal must be explicit; migration gating between two
processes; TRUNCATE versus row-level triggers; environment-dependent defaults
stated in health output.

### What broke
- The dry check's first real run chose D: 96% coverage beat B's 80% on OSRM
  roads with D at ~4.4 km. Fixed by moving D to ~7.4 km and widening the zone.
  The zone upsert had `update: {}` and would never have propagated that, so it
  now updates its geometry.
- `process.exit()` in the dry check aborted Node on Windows with a libuv
  assertion (exit 127). Switched to `process.exitCode`.
- `consoleRoutes` failed on its own: 199 leftover HIGH_RISK `console-job-a-*`
  jobs pushed its newest past `/queue?limit=200`. A pre-existing time bomb that
  about 200 runs set off; it now cancels its own leftovers first.
- `demoFlow` failed in parallel: it legitimately picked demo crew B, and the
  demo test's afterAll forced the held van OFFLINE. afterAll now skips busy
  vans, and an expired-but-ACTIVE hold no longer counts as busy.
- The migration wait logged "relation _prisma_migrations does not exist"
  because Prisma prints caught raw-query errors; it now checks to_regclass.
- The image did not contain the dry check the docs told people to run in it.
- `railway variable delete` has no --skip-deploys; each delete redeployed.

### Interview angle
"How do you keep a public demo from rotting?" Make the board ensure itself on
boot and refresh its clocks from the worker, skipping anything on a live job,
and give it a check that fails if the solver stops making the intended choice.

"Your demo picked the wrong van — what did you change?" The seed, not the cost
function: the far crew's wider coverage really was worth more at 4 km, so the
fixture was wrong, not the engine.

## Phase 27 — a six-act demo on the live board, without a wipe / 2026-10-17 / (this commit)

### Built
- `src/demo/demoCrews.ts` reworked: crew B a flatbed with ALL_CAPABILITIES and
  ALL_PARTS (derived from src/domain/faults.ts); A van JUMP_START only; C
  flatbed, every skill, no stock, ~3.3 km; D flatbed, everything, ~7.4 km;
  `canSignIn` (B only); `DEMO_WORKSHOP`; `DEMO_REFERENCE_DRIVER`.
- `src/demo/faultScenarios.ts` — one answer set per reachable fault (11; the
  classifier never makes KEY_FAILURE the top fault).
- `src/demo/demoScope.ts` — `demoJobWhere/UnitWhere/TechnicianWhere/
  DepotWhere/WorkshopWhere`, applied in `buildDispatchSnapshot` and GET /jobs,
  /queue, /events, /actions, /capabilities/scarcity, /degraded/payments.
- `src/demo/demoDispatch.ts` (`dispatchAfterIntake`, `redispatchAfterRefusal`),
  new trigger `INTAKE_COMPLETED`, `handleTrigger({ excludeTechnicianIds })`,
  `src/commit/excludeCrews.ts` `withoutTechnicians`; hooks in the intake
  routes, the offer respond route and the offer-expiry worker.
- `prisma/seed/demoReferenceJob.ts` `ensureReferenceJob`,
  `prisma/seed/demoDrivers.ts`, `prisma/seed/demoWorkshop.ts`.
- `src/demo/freeDemoVans.ts` (`freeStuckDemoVans`,
  `freeDemoVansAfterCompletion`), `src/demo/retireStaleDemoJobs.ts`,
  `src/demo/retireDemoJob.ts`; all run from `src/workers/demoRefresh.ts`.
- GET /login/accounts, `public/js/loginHints.js`, `public/js/pages/home.js`,
  `unusablePasswordHash`.
- `src/ai/usageLimits.ts` `checkAssistantAllowance`, config
  `AI_HOURLY_LIMIT_PER_IP` / `AI_DAILY_CALL_CAP`, `cappedResponse` on the
  describe route, driver screen shows the server's sentence.
- `scripts/demoJourney.ts` + dry check part 2 (real routes, every fault).
- Tests: demoScope, loginAccounts, demoDispatch, aiUsageLimits; demoBoard on
  its own database (`tests/helpers/demoDatabase.ts`) — idempotency with the
  reference job, cleanup spares a running van, stale-job retirement.

### Decisions
Decision: hide the full seed by filter in demo mode, never delete. Why: the
brief forbids wiping the live database. Alternative: park seeded vans OFFLINE.
Why not: rewrites 24 rows and has to be undone; depots and workshops have no
"off" state at all. Tradeoff: every dispatcher read and the snapshot gained a
`...demo*Where()` spread that is `{}` outside demo mode.

Decision: B a flatbed with everything. Why: the tow fault rejects vans; cost
ignores kind; the driver screen never reports a basement. Tradeoff: B now
holds every scarce capability pair, so on the shared zippy_test it stole
another suite's job — the demo tests moved to their own database.

Decision: the reference job ends RESOLVED on D after B declined. Why: an
in-progress job holds a van, and on A, C or D that spoils act 2's candidate
table or act 6's requeue target. Alternative: an EN_ROUTE job on C. Why not:
C would show "unit is on a job" instead of PART_UNAVAILABLE for every live job.

Decision: new trigger INTAKE_COMPLETED fired only in demo mode, card still
executed by a human. Why: a reported job had no path to an offer at all.
Alternative: auto-execute. Why not: the reservation stays a human step.

Decision: exclude the refusing technician from the re-plan after a refusal,
as a snapshot transform in src/commit. Why: otherwise the solver re-picks
them. Alternative: a rejection code in the engine. Why not: engine is
off-limits and "do not ask them again" is job history, a commit concern.
Tradeoff: the excluded crew vanishes from that job's candidate table instead
of showing as rejected.

Decision: stop on the tow rather than fake a workshop acceptance. Why: the
existing DESTINATION_UNCONFIRMABLE guard is correct — the product has no
workshop counterparty — and demoing an acceptance nobody gives would be a lie.

Decision: free demo vans after completion in demo mode only. Why: completion
never frees a van anywhere in the product; fixing that globally is a product
change outside the brief. Tradeoff: the bug stays for every other mode.

Decision: AI ceiling counted in Redis, failing closed. Why: counters are
cache-like (invariant 1); failing open could drain the key. Tradeoff: a Redis
outage costs every driver the assistant (they still get the picker).

### Concurrency
Scenario: the demo refresh frees or re-shifts a van while a dispatcher
reserves it. What races: freeStuckDemoVans' AVAILABLE write vs a new hold.
Why dangerous: two jobs on one van (invariant 7). How handled: busy check
first (live job or unexpired ACTIVE hold), then a conditional update
`WHERE status IN ('HELD','ON_JOB')`. Assumption relied on: a hold taken after
the busy read sets the unit HELD, which the conditional update then still
overwrites — the read-then-write window is narrow but real. Accepted for the
demo board.

Scenario: B declines while the offer-expiry worker times the same offer out.
What races: two re-plans for one job. How handled: advanceSaga returns
REQUEUED to both callers, so both may fire redispatchAfterRefusal; requestAction
then raises a second card — harmless but untidy. Not deduplicated.

### Weaknesses introduced
- Product-wide, found here: completion never frees a van (demo works around).
- Tows remain undispatchable; the dry check reports them as a known limitation.
- No price range before the estimate (act 1 asked for one).
- Act 6's card names D, but D has no login, so the act stops at the card.
- Live still runs Razorpay test keys unless the user deletes them (step 1).
- Retiring demo jobs is a demo-only CANCELLED path; there is still no product
  cancellation route.
- Double re-plan cards possible when decline and expiry race (above).
- None of the six acts walked in a browser on the deployed site.

### Concepts now genuinely in use
Feature-flagged read scoping ({}-or-clause), snapshot transforms in the commit
layer, per-job exclusion on re-plan, isolated test databases, fail-closed rate
limiting, driving the real HTTP surface in-process for an acceptance check.

### What broke
- The dry check first showed the seeded depots made C feasible everywhere;
  only the snapshot filter restored PART_UNAVAILABLE (measured: 11 FEASIBLE vs
  9 PART_UNAVAILABLE + 2 feasible).
- redispatch's "starts a real saga" failed: B (every capability) stole its job
  from the shared zippy_test — demo tests moved to zippy_test_demo.
- The first end-to-end tow run failed at Execute: DESTINATION_UNCONFIRMABLE.
  Investigated, kept as a documented limit.
- Walking acts 3-5 showed B's van stuck ON_JOB after completion: the product
  never frees a van on COMPLETE.
- The dry check never exited (in-process app's Redis/queue handles); then
  orphaned node processes from stopped tasks kept running on Windows and
  slowed later runs. Fixed with closeConnections + an unref'd exit backstop;
  orphans killed. Real run time: 9 s.
- Two test runs reported exit 1 with every test green and took 10+ minutes;
  not reproduced on rerun, likely the orphans above.

### Interview angle
"How do you demo on a production database you are not allowed to wipe?" Scope
every read the demo touches with a flag-controlled clause that is a no-op when
off, so hiding is a filter and undo is one variable.

"Your demo needed the solver to pick one crew for everything — did you tune
the engine?" No: the seed gives B every skill and part and the others a
visible flaw, and a dry check drives the real routes for all eleven faults and
fails if B ever loses.

## Phase 27, addendum — the browser walk / 2026-10-17 / (this commit)

### Built
Fixes only, each found by walking the six acts in a real browser on a local
copy of the live board (demo mode, Razorpay test keys, Groq assistant), then
switching the live deployment on: DEMO_MODE=true on web and worker, pushed,
redeployed; the dry check passed inside the live web container.

### Decisions
Decision: keep Razorpay test mode on, as the maintainer asked. Why: their
call. Consequence handled: the seed's reference job skips its payment when
Razorpay is configured (a seed cannot complete a checkout), and the docs say
the test checkout needs a real-format mobile number.

Decision: dispatcher board refetches on a short list of event types, debounced,
rather than on every event or only on Refresh. Why: the queue and action list
were Refresh-only by design ("a request per event" was the worry), which hid
a new breakdown from the dispatcher; the events that change them are rare.

### Concurrency
Scenario: the offer-expiry job fires for an offer already declined. What
races: two re-plans for one job. How handled: re-plan from the worker only
when timeoutOffer RECORDED this run's timeout. (Replaces the "double card"
weakness recorded above — it was not a rare race, it happened on every decline.)

### Weaknesses introduced
- The inspector heading shows the unit's internal id, not its code.
- "Pay now" stays on the driver's screen after completion when unpaid.
- The deployed site was not walked in a browser: Chrome showed "Security
  error" for the Railway domain (server answered 200 to curl).

### Concepts now genuinely in use
Event-type-filtered UI refetch; transition-only event writing.

### What broke
- The health banner said "Crew on shift degraded (7 of 28)" in demo mode: the
  probes counted the hidden full seed. Probes now use the demo filters.
- The reference job left a "nobody is assigned" card open forever (B's decline
  raises it; only the re-plan card was executed). The seed acknowledges it.
- The driver read "Your technician found the problem is not what we first
  thought" the moment a fresh job got its dispatch card. The replanning
  sentence now needs a crew actually being replaced (pre-existing for the
  safety-message card too).
- Dispatch cards never said who they send (nested ids, unrendered); the
  intake card was titled "Reassign". Now "sends: Ravi Kumar · DMO-VB" and
  "Dispatch — intake is complete".
- With Razorpay on, one open driver page wrote 2,521 PAYMENT_AUTHORISATION_PENDING
  events in six minutes: checkout asked on every refresh, each ask wrote an
  event, each event refreshed the page. Events now only on a transition.
- New jobs and cards did not appear on the dispatcher's board until Refresh.
- Every declined job got a second dispatch card at its 45 s deadline.
- The timeline's filter dropdowns listed the hidden seed's zones and events.
- The seed-twice test exceeded vitest's 5 s once the seed grew; given 30 s.

### Interview angle
"What did a browser find that 1,030 tests did not?" A payment event loop that
only exists with a real gateway's 'awaiting customer' answer, and a dispatcher
board that needed a manual refresh to show a new breakdown — both invisible to
any test that calls one route at a time.
